
            The Filename Completion Plugin for FAR
                   Version 1.3, 3 Jan 1999


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
DISTRIBUTION: Freeware. You may freely distribute this plugin without any
modifications if you make no commercial profit of distribution, keep
this read.me together with the plugin and leave it unchanged.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This plugin does the thing I've always been missing in FAR, that is,
command-line filename completion, like in 4DOS/4NT. And even more, it
completes host names and shared resource names, asking for password if needed.
And, since version 1.3, in FAR 1.60+, it works also in editor (this may be
useful for typing batch files etc.).

Copy FComplet.dll to your FAR directory, restart FAR, press F11, and you
will see the new "Filename Completion" menu item. Choose this item, and
you will see a menu of four filename completion commands:

Next          Get the next matching filename.
Previous      Return to the previous matching filename.
List...       List all the matching filenames.
Short names   Toggle using Short/Long filenames.

Of course, the plugin is useful only if you use key macros to call these
commands.

Wildcards *,? are supported; '*' is always assumed at the end. FAR's
extended wildcards with square brackets are not supported (and not needed
here, right?).
Plugins' virtual "directories" are not supported in this version either.

All slashes '/' are treated as backslashes. Everything said below is true
for slashes too.

If the path being expanded starts with two backslashes (\\), the plugin
does the following:

- if there are no more backslashes in the line, it attempts to find all
Windows network hosts matching the name after "\\", belonging to your domain
or workgroup;
- if there is exactly one more backslash in the line, it treats the word
between "\\" and "\" as a server name, and tries to find matching resource
names belonging to this host;
- if there are more backslashes, this is a UNC path and expanded as a usual
directory name.

In hostname/resource name completion, the plugin DOES understand FAR's
extended wildcards, in UNC directories it does NOT, as mentioned above.

Filenames typed at the start of the command line will match only subdirectories
and files with extensions .exe,.com,.bat,.cmd,.btm. Filenames typed after the
command "cd" will match only subdirectories. If you need to override these,
type a space at the command line start, that's enough.

You can use the plugin with any key assignments you like. 4DOS/4NT uses Tab
for this purpose. You probably wouldn't want to lose your current Tab key
functionality in FAR, so you have to use some other keys. Two possible
styles of key assignments are proposed.

(1) If you rarely type the '`' and '~' characters in command line, you can
assign them to do filename completion. It is convenient, as the `/~ key
is close to Tab. You can use Alt-`/Alt-Shift-` to input these characters
if you still need them. So you can create these macros:

  `             - find next;
  ~ (Shift-'`') - return to previous;
  Ctrl-`        - show list;
  Ctrl-Shift-N  - toggle the Short names mode.

These macros are provided in the FCOMPLET.RG1 file. I didn't do it for the
editor, because most people would prefer to leave the `/~ key in the editor
intact.

(2) Another proposed method is using Ctrl-Space assignments, that is:

  Ctrl-Space       - find next;
  Ctrl-Shift-Space - return to previous;
  Ctrl-Alt-Space   - show list.
  Ctrl-Shift-N  - toggle the Short names mode.

These macros are provided in the FCOMPLET.RG2 file. They work in editor too.

The files .rg1 and .rg2 are usual .reg files. You can import them using
REGEDIT or the Registry Browser plugin. If you do this from inside the FAR,
don't forget to restart FAR, but previously disable the Auto save setup option
(this is true for FAR 1.50).

ATTENTION! The FCOMPLET.RG? files assume that the hotkey auto-assigned to
this plugin in the Plugins menu, is 'i' in command line and 'f' in editor.
Check this first! Press F11 and check if the highlighted letters in
"Filename completion" item are as I assumed. If they are different, fix
these, or record your own macros.

